## Recursion

**Recursion** is a technique in which a function calls **itself**, directly or through another function, to solve a problem by reducing it to a smaller instance of the same problem. A function that does this is a **recursive function**.

Every recursive function needs:

• a **base case**: the simplest input, answered without another call (it stops the recursion)
• a **recursive case**: the call on a smaller input that moves towards the base case

Each call gets its own **stack frame** (parameters, locals, return address). Without a reachable base case the frames pile up until a **stack overflow**.

## Recursion example: factorial

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)               /* base case */
        return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);        /* recursive case */
}

int main(void) {
    int n = 5;                          /* or read it: scanf("%d", &n); */
    if (n < 0)
        printf("Factorial is not defined for negative numbers\n");
    else
        printf("%d! = %lld\n", n, factorial(n));
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
5! = 120
------------------------------------------

fact(5) = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × fact(1) = 120.

## Parameter passing

**Parameter passing** is the way the values written in a function call (**actual parameters**, or arguments) are handed to the variables in the function header (**formal parameters**). In add(x, y) with int add(int a, int b), x and y are actual, a and b are formal.

## Definitions

• **Call by value**: a **copy** of each argument is passed. The function works on its own copy, so the caller's variable never changes.
• **Call by reference**: the **address** of the argument is passed. The function reaches the caller's variable through it, so changes are seen by the caller. C does this with pointers: f(&x) and an int *p parameter (C++ also has true references, int &a).

## Parameter passing example

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

void byValue(int a)      { a = a + 5; }      /* gets a copy */
void byReference(int *a) { *a = *a + 5; }    /* gets the address */

int main(void) {
    int x = 10;
    byValue(x);
    printf("After call by value:     x = %d\n", x);
    byReference(&x);
    printf("After call by reference: x = %d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
After call by value:     x = 10
After call by reference: x = 15
------------------------------------------

x stays 10 after call by value and becomes 15 after call by reference.

Exam tip: this question has two parts; give each its own heading, definition, example and output.

