## Syntax of a function in C

------------------------------------------
return_type function_name(type param1, type param2, ...)
{
    /* local declarations */
    /* statements */
    return value;        /* omitted for void */
}
------------------------------------------

• **return_type**: the type of the value sent back (int, float, void if none).
• **function_name**: a valid identifier.
• **parameter list**: formal parameters with their types; void if none.
• **body**: statements inside { }.
• **return**: ends the function and sends back at most one value.

## The three parts in one program

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

int add(int a, int b);           /* 1. prototype (declaration) */

int main(void) {
    int s = add(5, 3);           /* 2. call: 5 and 3 are actual arguments */
    printf("Sum = %d\n", s);
    return 0;
}

int add(int a, int b) {          /* 3. definition: a, b are formal parameters */
    return a + b;                /* returns one value to the caller */
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
Sum = 8
------------------------------------------

Prototype (declaration) → call → definition. The prototype lets main call add before its definition appears.

