## Reading the loops

As printed the code has typos (a comma in for (int i=1,i<n;i++), capital For, an extra brace); the intended loops are:

------------------------------------------
for (i = 1; i < n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < i; j++)
        for (k = 0; k < i; k++)
            x = x + 1;
------------------------------------------

Each loop becomes one recursive function whose parameter is the loop counter and whose base case is the loop's exit test.

## Recursive version, checked against the loops

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

/* k loop: for (k = 0; k < i; k++) x = x + 1;  adds i */
int inner(int i, int k)  { return (k == i) ? 0 : 1 + inner(i, k + 1); }
/* j loop: for (j = 0; j < i; j++) runs the k loop i times */
int middle(int i, int j) { return (j == i) ? 0 : inner(i, 0) + middle(i, j + 1); }
/* i loop: for (i = 1; i < n; i++) */
int outer(int i, int n)  { return (i >= n) ? 0 : middle(i, 0) + outer(i + 1, n); }

int main(void) {
    int n = 5, x = 0, i, j, k;
    for (i = 1; i < n; i++)               /* the loops, as intended */
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++)
            for (k = 0; k < i; k++)
                x = x + 1;
    printf("loops: x = %d\n", x);
    printf("recursion: x = %d\n", outer(1, n));
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
loops: x = 30
recursion: x = 30
------------------------------------------

• inner(i, k) adds 1 for each k from 0 to i − 1, so it returns i.
• middle(i, j) calls inner i times, so it returns i × i.
• outer(1, n) adds i × i for i = 1 … n − 1: x = (n − 1)·n·(2n − 1) / 6 = **30** for n = 5.

