## Answer: (b) 40

A struct passed by value is copied **whole**, members included. The rule that arrays are passed by reference applies to an array passed on its own (it decays to a pointer); an array that is a **member of a struct** is copied with it.

int roll = 4 bytes, char name[28] = 28 bytes, double cgpa = 8 bytes → 4 + 28 + 8 = **40 bytes**.

## Check

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    int roll;
    char name[28];
    double cgpa;
} S;

void show(S s) {                   /* s is a full copy */
    s.roll = 99;                   /* changes only the copy */
}

int main(void) {
    S st = {7, "Rahim", 3.75};
    show(st);
    printf("sizeof(S) = %zu, roll after call = %d\n", sizeof(S), st.roll);
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
sizeof(S) = 40, roll after call = 7
------------------------------------------

The change inside show() does not reach st (roll stays 7), which confirms that a full copy was made. To avoid copying 40 bytes, pass const S *.

