answer.en:
## Definitions

• **Recursion**: a function repeats work by calling itself until a base case.
• **Iteration**: a loop (for, while) repeats a block until its condition becomes false.

## Stack usage

Every function call pushes a **stack frame** (parameters, local variables, return address). A recursive function has one frame for **every call still waiting**, so its stack use grows with the depth of recursion. A loop runs inside a single frame and reuses the same variables.

## Example: factorial both ways, counting frames

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

int depth = 0, maxDepth = 0;

long long factRec(int n) {
    depth++;
    if (depth > maxDepth) maxDepth = depth;
    long long r = (n <= 1) ? 1 : n * factRec(n - 1);
    depth--;
    return r;
}

long long factLoop(int n) {
    long long f = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) f *= i;
    return f;
}

int main(void) {
    printf("recursive 20! = %lld, frames at once = %d\n", factRec(20), maxDepth);
    printf("iterative 20! = %lld, frames at once = 1\n", factLoop(20));
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
recursive 20! = 2432902008176640000, frames at once = 20
iterative 20! = 2432902008176640000, frames at once = 1
------------------------------------------

## Comparison

Point | Recursion | Iteration
Stops when | the base case is reached | the loop condition becomes false
Stack memory | O(depth): 20 frames for fact(20) | O(1): one frame
Overhead | a call and return per step | none beyond the loop test
Failure | stack overflow (no or unreachable base case) | infinite loop
Code | short and close to the definition | sometimes longer, needs explicit state
Best for | trees, Tower of Hanoi, divide-and-conquer | counting, simple sums, scanning arrays

• Naive recursive Fibonacci needs only O(n) stack but O(2ⁿ) time, because it repeats calls (15 calls for fib(5)); the loop takes O(n) time and O(1) memory.
• Tail recursion (return gcd(b, a % b);) can be turned into a loop by an optimising compiler, which removes the extra frames.

Exam tip: draw the stack for fact(4) (four frames, then popping 1, 2, 6, 24) next to the loop's single frame.

