## Recursive digit separation

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

void printDigits(int n) {
    if (n >= 10)                 /* more than one digit left */
        printDigits(n / 10);     /* first print the leading digits */
    printf("%d ", n % 10);       /* then this (last) digit */
}

int main(void) {
    int n = 5396;
    printf("Digits of %d: ", n);
    printDigits(n);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

## Output

------------------------------------------
Digits of 5396: 5 3 9 6
------------------------------------------

• printDigits(5396) first calls printDigits(539), which calls printDigits(53), then printDigits(5).
• 5 has one digit, so it is printed; then each waiting call prints its last digit: 3, 9, 6.
• Because printf comes **after** the recursive call, the digits come out left to right. Printing before the call would give 6 9 3 5.

